<section xml:id="sec-arithmetic-foundations">
  <title>Arithmetic Foundations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Prerequisite.</em>
      Familiarity with the mathematical language and notation introduced in
      <xref ref="sec-mathematical-language-notation"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li><p>interpret addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division;</p></li>
      <li><p>use the commutative, associative, distributive, identity, and inverse properties;</p></li>
      <li><p>perform arithmetic with positive and negative numbers;</p></li>
      <li><p>work with fractions and mixed arithmetic expressions;</p></li>
      <li><p>use ratios, rates, proportions, and percentages;</p></li>
      <li><p>apply exponent laws and interpret negative and rational exponents;</p></li>
      <li><p>simplify and interpret radicals;</p></li>
      <li><p>use scientific notation;</p></li>
      <li><p>apply the standard order of operations;</p></li>
      <li><p>estimate quantities and check whether calculations are reasonable;</p></li>
      <li><p>recognize common arithmetic errors that later affect algebra and calculus.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arithmetic-structure">
    <title>Arithmetic and Mathematical Structure</title>

    <p>Arithmetic studies numbers and the operations performed on them. At an elementary level, arithmetic provides methods for calculation. At a deeper level, however, arithmetic introduces structural properties that reappear throughout algebra, analysis, number theory, and other branches of mathematics.</p>

    <p>The four fundamental arithmetic operations are:</p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>addition;</p></li>
        <li><p>subtraction;</p></li>
        <li><p>multiplication;</p></li>
        <li><p>division.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>These operations do not behave independently. They are connected through inverse operations and governed by properties such as commutativity, associativity, and distributivity.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-addition">
    <title>Addition</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Addition</title>
      <statement>
        <p><em>Addition</em> combines quantities to produce a quantity called their <em>sum</em>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The expression</p>

    <p>
      <md>a+b</md>
    </p>

    <p>is read <q><m>a</m> plus <m>b</m>.</q></p>

    <p>The symbol <m>+</m> is the <em>plus sign</em>.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>7+5=12.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Here <m>7</m> and <m>5</m> are called <em>addends</em>, and <m>12</m> is their sum.</p>

    <p>Addition may represent combining collections, increasing a quantity, accumulating change, or combining mathematical objects with an appropriate addition operation.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-subtraction">
    <title>Subtraction</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Subtraction</title>
      <statement>
        <p><em>Subtraction</em> finds the difference between quantities and may be interpreted as addition of an additive inverse.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The expression</p>

    <p>
      <md>a-b</md>
    </p>

    <p>is read <q><m>a</m> minus <m>b</m>.</q></p>

    <p>The symbol <m>-</m> is the <em>minus sign</em>.</p>

    <p>The first quantity is the <em>minuend</em>, the second is the <em>subtrahend</em>, and the result is the <em>difference</em>.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>11-4=7.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Subtraction can also be written as addition:</p>

    <p>
      <md>a-b=a+(-b).</md>
    </p>

    <p>This interpretation becomes particularly important in algebra.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-minus-sign-roles">
    <title>The Minus Sign Has Multiple Roles</title>

    <p>The minus sign can represent several related ideas.</p>

    <p>In</p>

    <p>
      <md>8-3,</md>
    </p>

    <p>it indicates subtraction.</p>

    <p>In</p>

    <p>
      <md>-5,</md>
    </p>

    <p>it indicates the opposite, or additive inverse, of <m>5</m>.</p>

    <p>In</p>

    <p>
      <md>-x,</md>
    </p>

    <p>it represents the additive inverse of <m>x</m>.</p>

    <p>Therefore, the same written symbol must sometimes be interpreted according to its position in an expression.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-multiplication">
    <title>Multiplication</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Multiplication</title>
      <statement>
        <p><em>Multiplication</em> combines quantities through repeated addition, scaling, or a more general product operation.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Multiplication may be written in several forms:</p>

    <p>
      <md>a\times b,\qquad a\cdot b,\qquad ab.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The symbol <m>\times</m> is the <em>multiplication sign</em>, and <m>\cdot</m> is the <em>multiplication dot</em>.</p>

    <p>The quantities being multiplied are called <em>factors</em>. Their result is called the <em>product</em>.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>4\cdot7=28.</md>
    </p>

    <p>In algebra, multiplication is often written by juxtaposition:</p>

    <p>
      <md>4x=4\cdot x.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-multiplication-scaling">
    <title>Multiplication as Scaling</title>

    <p>Multiplication is more general than repeated addition.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac12(8)=4</md>
    </p>

    <p>scales <m>8</m> by a factor of one-half.</p>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>1.5(10)=15</md>
    </p>

    <p>scales <m>10</m> by a factor of <m>1.5</m>.</p>

    <p>This interpretation becomes especially useful when studying functions, geometry, vectors, probability, and linear algebra.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-division">
    <title>Division</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Division</title>
      <statement>
        <p><em>Division</em> determines how one quantity is distributed relative to another nonzero quantity and may be interpreted as multiplication by a reciprocal.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Division may be written as</p>

    <p>
      <md>a\div b,\qquad \frac ab,\qquad a/b.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The quantity being divided is the <em>dividend</em>, the quantity dividing it is the <em>divisor</em>, and the result is the <em>quotient</em>.</p>

    <p>For <m>b\neq0</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab=a\left(\frac1b\right).</md>
    </p>

    <p>Thus division by <m>b</m> is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal of <m>b</m>.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-division-zero">
    <title>Division by Zero</title>

    <p>Division by zero is undefined in ordinary arithmetic.</p>

    <p>Suppose one attempted to define</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac10=x.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Multiplying both sides by <m>0</m> would suggest</p>

    <p>
      <md>1=0x=0,</md>
    </p>

    <p>which is impossible.</p>

    <p>Therefore,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac10</md>
    </p>

    <p>has no ordinary real or complex value.</p>

    <warning>
      <title>Division by Zero</title>
      <p>A denominator may never equal zero in ordinary arithmetic.</p>
      <p>The expression</p>
      <p>
        <md>\frac{a}{0}</md>
      </p>
      <p>is undefined.</p>
      <p>The expression <m>0/0</m> is also undefined. In calculus, <m>0/0</m> may appear as an <em>indeterminate form</em> in a limit, but this does not mean that division by zero becomes valid.</p>
    </warning>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-inverse-operations">
    <title>Inverse Operations</title>

    <p>Addition and subtraction are inverse operations.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>7+4=11</md>
    </p>

    <p>can be reversed by</p>

    <p>
      <md>11-4=7.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Multiplication and division are also inverse operations when the divisor is nonzero:</p>

    <p>
      <md>5\cdot6=30,\qquad 30\div6=5.</md>
    </p>

    <p>This idea of reversing an operation later develops into inverse functions and inverse algebraic operations.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-commutative-property">
    <title>The Commutative Property</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Commutative Property</title>
      <statement>
        <p>An operation is <em>commutative</em> when changing the order of the operands does not change the result.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Addition is commutative:</p>

    <p>
      <md>a+b=b+a.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Multiplication is commutative:</p>

    <p>
      <md>ab=ba.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>4+9=9+4,\qquad 3\cdot8=8\cdot3.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Subtraction is generally not commutative:</p>

    <p>
      <md>8-3\neq3-8.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Division is generally not commutative:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac84\neq\frac48.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-associative-property">
    <title>The Associative Property</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Associative Property</title>
      <statement>
        <p>An operation is <em>associative</em> when changing the grouping of repeated operations does not change the result.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Addition is associative:</p>

    <p>
      <md>(a+b)+c=a+(b+c).</md>
    </p>

    <p>Multiplication is associative:</p>

    <p>
      <md>(ab)c=a(bc).</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>(2+3)+4=2+(3+4)=9.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Subtraction and division are generally not associative:</p>

    <p>
      <md>(10-5)-2\neq10-(5-2).</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-distributive-property">
    <title>The Distributive Property</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Distributive Property</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The distributive property connects multiplication with addition or subtraction.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For addition,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a(b+c)=ab+ac.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For subtraction,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a(b-c)=ab-ac.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>3(5+2)=3(5)+3(2)=15+6=21.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The distributive property is one of the most frequently used algebraic properties in mathematics.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-identity-elements">
    <title>Identity Elements</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Additive Identity</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The <em>additive identity</em> is a number that leaves another number unchanged under addition.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For ordinary arithmetic, the additive identity is <m>0</m>:</p>

    <p>
      <md>a+0=0+a=a.</md>
    </p>

    <definition>
      <title>Multiplicative Identity</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The <em>multiplicative identity</em> is a number that leaves another number unchanged under multiplication.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For ordinary arithmetic, the multiplicative identity is <m>1</m>:</p>

    <p>
      <md>a\cdot1=1\cdot a=a.</md>
    </p>

    <p>These concepts later generalize to identity elements in abstract algebra.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-additive-inverses">
    <title>Additive Inverses</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Additive Inverse</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The <em>additive inverse</em> of <m>a</m> is the number <m>-a</m> satisfying</p>
        <p>
          <md>a+(-a)=0.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Examples include</p>

    <p>
      <md>5+(-5)=0,\qquad -12+12=0.</md>
    </p>

    <p>A number and its additive inverse are often called <em>opposites</em>.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-multiplicative-inverses">
    <title>Multiplicative Inverses</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Multiplicative Inverse</title>
      <statement>
        <p>For <m>a\neq0</m>, the <em>multiplicative inverse</em>, or <em>reciprocal</em>, of <m>a</m> is <m>1/a</m>, because</p>
        <p>
          <md>a\left(\frac1a\right)=1.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For example, the reciprocal of <m>5</m> is <m>1/5</m>, while the reciprocal of <m>2/3</m> is <m>3/2</m>.</p>

    <p>Zero has no multiplicative inverse.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-zero-properties">
    <title>Zero Properties</title>

    <p>Several basic properties of zero are used constantly:</p>

    <p>
      <md>a+0=a,\qquad a-0=a,\qquad a\cdot0=0.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For <m>a\neq0</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac0a=0.</md>
    </p>

    <p>However,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac a0</md>
    </p>

    <p>is undefined.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-signed-numbers">
    <title>Positive and Negative Numbers</title>

    <p>A positive number is greater than zero, while a negative number is less than zero.</p>

    <p>Thus,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a&gt;0</md>
    </p>

    <p>means <m>a</m> is positive, and</p>

    <p>
      <md>a&lt;0</md>
    </p>

    <p>means <m>a</m> is negative.</p>

    <p>Zero is neither positive nor negative.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-signed-addition">
    <title>Addition with Signed Numbers</title>

    <p>When two numbers have the same sign, add their magnitudes and keep the common sign:</p>

    <p>
      <md>5+3=8,\qquad (-5)+(-3)=-8.</md>
    </p>

    <p>When the signs differ, subtract the smaller magnitude from the larger and use the sign of the number having greater magnitude:</p>

    <p>
      <md>8+(-3)=5,\qquad 3+(-8)=-5.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-signed-subtraction">
    <title>Subtraction with Signed Numbers</title>

    <p>Subtraction can always be rewritten as addition of the opposite:</p>

    <p>
      <md>a-b=a+(-b).</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>7-(-4)=7+4=11.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>-3-5=-3+(-5)=-8.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-signed-multiplication">
    <title>Multiplication and Division with Signed Numbers</title>

    <p>The sign rules for multiplication are</p>

    <p>
      <md>(+)(+)=+,\qquad (+)(-)=-,\qquad (-)(+)=-,\qquad (-)(-)=+.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The same sign rules apply to division.</p>

    <p>Thus,</p>

    <p>
      <md>(-4)(-6)=24,\qquad (-20)\div5=-4.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arithmetic-absolute-value">
    <title>Absolute Value</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Absolute Value</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The <em>absolute value</em> of a real number <m>x</m>, written <m>\left|x\right|</m>, is its distance from zero on the real number line.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The vertical bars in</p>

    <p>
      <md>\left|x\right|</md>
    </p>

    <p>are called <em>absolute-value bars</em> in this context.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\left|5\right|=5,\qquad \left|-5\right|=5,\qquad \left|0\right|=0.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Algebraically,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\left|x\right|=\begin{cases}x,&amp;x\geq0,\\-x,&amp;x&lt;0.\end{cases}</md>
    </p>

    <p>Absolute value is developed further as a distance concept in the section on number systems.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arithmetic-fractions">
    <title>Fractions</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Fraction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A <em>fraction</em> is an expression of the form</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac ab,\qquad b\neq0.</md>
        </p>
        <p>The quantity <m>a</m> is the numerator and <m>b</m> is the denominator.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The fraction bar represents division:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab=a\div b.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Fractions can represent parts of a whole, ratios, quotients, probabilities, rates, or exact numerical values.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-equivalent-fractions">
    <title>Equivalent Fractions</title>

    <p>Multiplying numerator and denominator by the same nonzero number does not change a fraction's value:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab=\frac{ka}{kb},\qquad k\neq0.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac12=\frac24=\frac36=\frac{50}{100}.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-reducing-fractions">
    <title>Reducing Fractions</title>

    <p>A fraction is in <em>lowest terms</em> when its numerator and denominator have no common factor greater than <m>1</m>.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{18}{24}=\frac34.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The numerator and denominator were both divided by <m>6</m>.</p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Cancellation</title>
      <p>Cancellation is division by a common nonzero <em>factor</em>.</p>
      <p>It does not apply across addition.</p>
      <p>For example,</p>
      <p>
        <md>\frac{6x}{6y}=\frac xy</md>
      </p>
      <p>when the denominators involved are nonzero, but</p>
      <p>
        <md>\frac{x+6}{x}</md>
      </p>
      <p>does not allow the <m>x</m>'s to be canceled because the numerator is a sum, not a product having <m>x</m> as a common factor.</p>
    </assemblage>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-adding-fractions">
    <title>Adding Fractions</title>

    <p>Fractions require a common denominator before their numerators can be added.</p>

    <p>If the denominators are equal,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab+\frac cb=\frac{a+c}{b}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For different nonzero denominators,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab+\frac cd=\frac{ad+bc}{bd}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Usually a least common denominator is preferred when convenient.</p>

    <example>
      <title>Adding Fractions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac34+\frac56.</md>
        </p>
        <table>
          <title>Adding Fractions Steps</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Find a common denominator.</cell>
              <cell><m>\operatorname{lcm}(4,6)=12</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite each fraction.</cell>
              <cell><m>\frac34=\frac9{12},\qquad \frac56=\frac{10}{12}</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Add the numerators.</cell>
              <cell><m>\frac9{12}+\frac{10}{12}=\frac{19}{12}</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>\frac{19}{12}</md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-subtracting-fractions">
    <title>Subtracting Fractions</title>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab-\frac cd=\frac{ad-bc}{bd}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac56-\frac14=\frac{10}{12}-\frac3{12}=\frac7{12}.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-multiplying-fractions">
    <title>Multiplying Fractions</title>

    <p>Fractions are multiplied by multiplying numerators and denominators:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab\cdot\frac cd=\frac{ac}{bd}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac23\cdot\frac9{10}=\frac{18}{30}=\frac35.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Common factors may often be canceled before multiplication.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-dividing-fractions">
    <title>Dividing Fractions</title>

    <p>Dividing by a nonzero fraction is equivalent to multiplying by its reciprocal:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab\div\frac cd=\frac ab\cdot\frac dc=\frac{ad}{bc}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>This requires</p>

    <p>
      <md>b\neq0,\qquad c\neq0,\qquad d\neq0.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-fractions">
    <title>Complex Fractions</title>

    <p>A <em>complex fraction</em> is a fraction containing fractions in its numerator, denominator, or both.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{\frac23}{\frac57}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>This can be simplified using division of fractions:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{\frac23}{\frac57}=\frac23\cdot\frac75=\frac{14}{15}.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ratios">
    <title>Ratios</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Ratio</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A <em>ratio</em> compares two quantities through division.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The ratio of <m>a</m> to <m>b</m> may be written</p>

    <p>
      <md>a:b</md>
    </p>

    <p>or</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The colon <m> : </m> is called a <em>colon</em>. In ratio notation, <m>a:b</m> is read <q><m>a</m> to <m>b</m>.</q></p>

    <p>For example, a class containing <m>12</m> students in one group and <m>8</m> in another has ratio</p>

    <p>
      <md>12:8=3:2.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rates">
    <title>Rates</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Rate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A <em>rate</em> is a ratio comparing quantities measured in different units.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Examples include</p>

    <p>
      <md>60\text{ miles}/2\text{ hours},\qquad \$120/8\text{ hours}.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-unit-rates">
    <title>Unit Rates</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Unit Rate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A <em>unit rate</em> is a rate whose second quantity has numerical value <m>1</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{180\text{ miles}}{3\text{ hours}}=60\text{ miles per hour}.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-proportions">
    <title>Proportions</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Proportion</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A <em>proportion</em> is an equation stating that two ratios are equal.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac23=\frac{x}{12}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For nonzero denominators,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac ab=\frac cd</md>
    </p>

    <p>implies</p>

    <p>
      <md>ad=bc.</md>
    </p>

    <p>This follows by multiplying both sides by <m>bd</m>.</p>

    <example>
      <title>Solving a Proportion</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Solve</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac35=\frac{x}{20}.</md>
        </p>
        <table>
          <title>Solving a Proportion Steps</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Cross multiply.</cell>
              <cell><m>3(20)=5x</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell><m>60=5x</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Divide by <m>5</m>.</cell>
              <cell><m>x=12</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>x=12</md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-percentages">
    <title>Percentages</title>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>\%</md>
    </p>

    <p>is called the <em>percent sign</em>.</p>

    <p>The word <em>percent</em> means <q>per hundred.</q></p>

    <p>Thus,</p>

    <p>
      <md>35\%=\frac{35}{100}=0.35.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>7\%=0.07,\qquad 125\%=1.25.</md>
    </p>

    <p>A percentage may be greater than <m>100\%</m>.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-percentage-quantity">
    <title>Finding a Percentage of a Quantity</title>

    <p>To find <m>p\%</m> of a quantity <m>A</m>, convert the percentage to a decimal or fraction and multiply:</p>

    <p>
      <md>p\%\text{ of }A=\frac{p}{100}A.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>15\%\text{ of }80=0.15(80)=12.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-percent-change">
    <title>Percent Increase and Decrease</title>

    <p>If a quantity changes from an original value <m>O\neq0</m> to a new value <m>N</m>, then</p>

    <p>
      <md>\text{percent change}=\frac{N-O}{O}\times100\%.</md>
    </p>

    <p>A positive result represents an increase and a negative result represents a decrease.</p>

    <example>
      <title>Percent Increase</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A quantity increases from <m>80</m> to <m>92</m>.</p>
        <table>
          <title>Percent Increase Steps</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Find the change.</cell>
              <cell><m>92-80=12</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Divide by the original value.</cell>
              <cell><m>\frac{12}{80}=0.15</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Convert to percent.</cell>
              <cell><m>0.15(100\%)=15\%</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>The quantity increased by <m>15\%</m>.</p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-percentage-points">
    <title>Percentage Points</title>

    <p>A change between percentages can be described in <em>percentage points</em>.</p>

    <p>For example, an increase from <m>20\%</m> to <m>25\%</m> is an increase of</p>

    <p>
      <md>25\%-20\%=5</md>
    </p>

    <p>percentage points.</p>

    <p>This is different from the relative percent increase:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{25-20}{20}\times100\%=25\%.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-exponents">
    <title>Exponents</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Exponent</title>
      <statement>
        <p>An <em>exponent</em> indicates repeated multiplication of a base.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For positive integer <m>n</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a^n=\underbrace{a\cdot a\cdots a}_{n\text{ factors}}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>In <m>a^n</m>, <m>a</m> is the <em>base</em> and <m>n</m> is the <em>exponent</em>.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>2^4=2\cdot2\cdot2\cdot2=16.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-exponent-product-rule">
    <title>Product Rule for Exponents</title>

    <p>When multiplying powers having the same base,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a^ma^n=a^{m+n}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>x^3x^5=x^8.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The exponents are added because the total number of factors of <m>x</m> is <m>3+5</m>.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-exponent-quotient-rule">
    <title>Quotient Rule for Exponents</title>

    <p>For <m>a\neq0</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{x^7}{x^3}=x^4,\qquad x\neq0.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-power-of-power">
    <title>Power of a Power</title>

    <p>
      <md>(a^m)^n=a^{mn}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>(x^3)^4=x^{12}.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-power-product">
    <title>Power of a Product</title>

    <p>
      <md>(ab)^n=a^nb^n.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>(2x)^3=2^3x^3=8x^3.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-power-quotient">
    <title>Power of a Quotient</title>

    <p>For <m>b\neq0</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\left(\frac ab\right)^n=\frac{a^n}{b^n}.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-zero-exponent">
    <title>The Zero Exponent</title>

    <p>For <m>a\neq0</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a^0=1.</md>
    </p>

    <p>This is consistent with the quotient rule:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{a^n}{a^n}=a^{n-n}=a^0=1.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The restriction <m>a\neq0</m> is important.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-negative-exponents">
    <title>Negative Exponents</title>

    <p>For <m>a\neq0</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a^{-n}=\frac1{a^n}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>2^{-3}=\frac1{2^3}=\frac18.</md>
    </p>

    <p>A negative exponent does not make a quantity negative. Instead, it indicates a reciprocal.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-common-exponent-errors">
    <title>Common Exponent Errors</title>

    <p>Exponent rules apply to multiplication, not generally to addition.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>(a+b)^2\neq a^2+b^2.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Instead,</p>

    <p>
      <md>(a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a^m+a^n</md>
    </p>

    <p>cannot generally be simplified by adding the exponents.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-radicals">
    <title>Radicals</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Radical</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A <em>radical expression</em> uses a root operation, commonly written with the radical symbol <m>\sqrt{\phantom{x}}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The square root of <m>a</m> is written</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt a.</md>
    </p>

    <p>More generally,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt[n]{a}</md>
    </p>

    <p>denotes an <m>n</m>-th root.</p>

    <p>The quantity <m>a</m> inside the radical is called the <em>radicand</em>.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-principal-square-roots">
    <title>Principal Square Roots</title>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt a</md>
    </p>

    <p>denotes the nonnegative square root when <m>a\geq0</m>.</p>

    <p>Thus,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt9=3,</md>
    </p>

    <p>not <m>\pm3</m>.</p>

    <p>However, the equation</p>

    <p>
      <md>x^2=9</md>
    </p>

    <p>has two real solutions:</p>

    <p>
      <md>x=\pm3.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The symbol <m>\pm</m> is the <em>plus-or-minus sign</em> and is read <q>plus or minus.</q></p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-square-root-square">
    <title>Square Roots of Squares</title>

    <p>For real <m>a</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt{a^2}=\left|a\right|.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The absolute value is necessary because the principal square root is nonnegative.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt{(-5)^2}=\sqrt{25}=5=\left|-5\right|.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-radical-product-property">
    <title>Product Property of Radicals</title>

    <p>For nonnegative real numbers <m>a,b</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt{ab}=\sqrt a\sqrt b.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt{36\cdot2}=6\sqrt2.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-simplifying-radicals">
    <title>Simplifying Radicals</title>

    <p>Perfect-power factors can be removed from radicals.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt{72}=\sqrt{36\cdot2}=6\sqrt2.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-radical-addition-error">
    <title>Radicals Do Not Distribute over Addition</title>

    <p>In general,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt{a+b}\neq\sqrt a+\sqrt b.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt{9+16}=5,</md>
    </p>

    <p>while</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt9+\sqrt{16}=3+4=7.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rational-exponents">
    <title>Rational Exponents</title>

    <p>Roots can be represented using fractional exponents.</p>

    <p>For suitable values,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a^{1/n}=\sqrt[n]{a}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>More generally,</p>

    <p>
      <md>a^{m/n}=\sqrt[n]{a^m}=\left(\sqrt[n]{a}\right)^m.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>27^{2/3}=\left(\sqrt[3]{27}\right)^2=3^2=9.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Domain restrictions depend on whether the root index is even or odd and on the number system being used.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-scientific-notation">
    <title>Scientific Notation</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Scientific Notation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A nonzero number is written in <em>scientific notation</em> in the form</p>
        <p>
          <md>a\times10^n,\qquad 1\leq\left|a\right|&lt;10,</md>
        </p>
        <p>where <m>n\in\mathbb{Z}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The multiplication sign <m>\times</m> is commonly used here because juxtaposition could make the decimal coefficient and power of ten difficult to distinguish.</p>

    <p>Examples include</p>

    <p>
      <md>4500000=4.5\times10^6</md>
    </p>

    <p>and</p>

    <p>
      <md>0.00032=3.2\times10^{-4}.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-scientific-notation-operations">
    <title>Operations in Scientific Notation</title>

    <p>To multiply quantities in scientific notation, multiply their coefficients and add the powers of ten:</p>

    <p>
      <md>(a\times10^m)(b\times10^n)=ab\times10^{m+n}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>(3\times10^4)(2\times10^5)=6\times10^9.</md>
    </p>

    <p>For division,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{a\times10^m}{b\times10^n}=\frac ab\times10^{m-n},\qquad b\neq0.</md>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-order-operations">
    <title>Order of Operations</title>

    <p>When an expression contains several operations, a standard convention determines the order in which they are performed.</p>

    <p>A useful order is:</p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>evaluate grouping symbols;</p></li>
        <li><p>evaluate exponents and roots;</p></li>
        <li><p>perform multiplication and division from left to right;</p></li>
        <li><p>perform addition and subtraction from left to right.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>Multiplication and division have equal priority.</p>

    <p>Addition and subtraction also have equal priority.</p>

    <p>Therefore, operations of equal priority are evaluated from left to right.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-order-operations-error">
    <title>A Common Order-of-Operations Error</title>

    <p>Consider</p>

    <p>
      <md>24\div6\cdot2.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Division and multiplication have equal priority, so evaluate from left to right:</p>

    <p>
      <md>24\div6\cdot2=4\cdot2=8.</md>
    </p>

    <p>It is incorrect to automatically perform every multiplication before every division.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-negative-exponent-precedence">
    <title>Unary Negatives and Exponents</title>

    <p>Consider</p>

    <p>
      <md>-3^2.</md>
    </p>

    <p>The exponent applies to <m>3</m> before the leading negative:</p>

    <p>
      <md>-3^2=-(3^2)=-9.</md>
    </p>

    <p>By contrast,</p>

    <p>
      <md>(-3)^2=9.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Parentheses change the base being squared.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-order-operations">
    <title>Worked Example: Order of Operations</title>

    <example>
      <title>Evaluate an Arithmetic Expression</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate</p>
        <p>
          <md>3+4(2^2)-\frac{6}{3}.</md>
        </p>
        <table>
          <title>Evaluate an Arithmetic Expression Steps</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Evaluate the exponent.</cell>
              <cell><m>3+4(4)-\frac63</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply and divide.</cell>
              <cell><m>3+16-2</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Add and subtract left to right.</cell>
              <cell><m>19-2=17</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>17</md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-exact-decimal-values">
    <title>Exact Values and Decimal Approximations</title>

    <p>Whenever practical, mathematics often preserves an exact value until an approximation is actually needed.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac13</md>
    </p>

    <p>is exact, whereas</p>

    <p>
      <md>0.333</md>
    </p>

    <p>is only an approximation.</p>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt2</md>
    </p>

    <p>is exact, while</p>

    <p>
      <md>\sqrt2\approx1.41421.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Early rounding can introduce accumulated error into later calculations.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rounding">
    <title>Rounding</title>

    <p>Rounding replaces a number by a nearby value having fewer digits.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>3.14159265\approx3.142</md>
    </p>

    <p>to three decimal places.</p>

    <p>The symbol <m>\approx</m> is appropriate because the rounded value is not exactly equal to the original quantity.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-estimation">
    <title>Estimation</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Estimation</title>
      <statement>
        <p><em>Estimation</em> replaces quantities by nearby, easier values to obtain an approximate result or check the reasonableness of an exact calculation.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>19.8(5.1)\approx20(5)=100.</md>
    </p>

    <p>Therefore, an exact calculator result near <m>100</m> is reasonable.</p>

    <p>A result such as <m>1000</m> would signal a likely arithmetic or input error.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arithmetic-units">
    <title>Units in Arithmetic</title>

    <p>Quantities with units require additional care.</p>

    <p>Compatible quantities can be added:</p>

    <p>
      <md>3\text{ m}+5\text{ m}=8\text{ m}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>But quantities with incompatible dimensions cannot ordinarily be added:</p>

    <p>
      <md>3\text{ m}+5\text{ s}</md>
    </p>

    <p>has no standard physical meaning.</p>

    <p>Multiplication and division combine units:</p>

    <p>
      <md>\frac{120\text{ miles}}{2\text{ hours}}=60\text{ miles/hour}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>This principle becomes important in applied mathematics and mathematical modeling.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arithmetic-properties-summary">
    <title>Arithmetic Properties Summary</title>

    <p>For suitable numbers,</p>

    <table>
      <title>Arithmetic Properties</title>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Property</cell>
          <cell>Form</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Commutative addition</cell>
          <cell><m>a+b=b+a</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Commutative multiplication</cell>
          <cell><m>ab=ba</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Associative addition</cell>
          <cell><m>(a+b)+c=a+(b+c)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Associative multiplication</cell>
          <cell><m>(ab)c=a(bc)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Distributive property</cell>
          <cell><m>a(b+c)=ab+ac</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Additive identity</cell>
          <cell><m>a+0=a</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Multiplicative identity</cell>
          <cell><m>a\cdot1=a</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Additive inverse</cell>
          <cell><m>a+(-a)=0</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Multiplicative inverse</cell>
          <cell><m>a(1/a)=1,\ a\neq0</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>These properties are not merely computational rules. They provide an early example of the structural laws later studied abstractly in algebra.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-common-arithmetic-errors">
    <title>Common Arithmetic Errors</title>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Adding Denominators</title>
      <p>In general,</p>
      <p>
        <md>\frac ab+\frac cd\neq\frac{a+c}{b+d}.</md>
      </p>
      <p>A common denominator is required.</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Canceling Terms Instead of Factors</title>
      <p>Cancellation applies to common factors, not pieces separated by addition or subtraction.</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Distributing Exponents over Addition</title>
      <p>In general,</p>
      <p>
        <md>(a+b)^n\neq a^n+b^n.</md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Ignoring Parentheses</title>
      <p>The expressions</p>
      <p>
        <md>-x^2 \qquad\text{and}\qquad (-x)^2</md>
      </p>
      <p>are different.</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Dividing by Zero</title>
      <p>Any algebraic manipulation that divides by a quantity requires checking that the quantity is nonzero.</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Rounding Too Early</title>
      <p>Repeated approximation may produce a noticeably inaccurate final result. Keep exact values when possible and round near the end of a calculation.</p>
    </paragraphs>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arithmetic-worked-examples">
    <title>Worked Examples</title>

    <example>
      <title>Simplifying a Fraction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac{84}{126}.</md>
        </p>
        <table>
          <title>Simplifying a Fraction Steps</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Find the greatest common factor.</cell>
              <cell><m>\gcd(84,126)=42</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Divide numerator and denominator.</cell>
              <cell><m>\frac{84\div42}{126\div42}</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell><m>\frac23</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>\frac23</md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Using Exponent Laws</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac{x^7x^2}{x^4},\qquad x\neq0.</md>
        </p>
        <table>
          <title>Using Exponent Laws Steps</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine the numerator.</cell>
              <cell><m>x^7x^2=x^9</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the quotient rule.</cell>
              <cell><m>\frac{x^9}{x^4}=x^{9-4}</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell><m>x^5</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>x^5</md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Simplifying a Radical</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify</p>
        <p>
          <md>\sqrt{180}.</md>
        </p>
        <table>
          <title>Simplifying a Radical Steps</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Find a perfect-square factor.</cell>
              <cell><m>180=36\cdot5</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Separate the radical.</cell>
              <cell><m>\sqrt{180}=\sqrt{36}\sqrt5</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Evaluate the perfect square.</cell>
              <cell><m>6\sqrt5</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>6\sqrt5</md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Scientific Notation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Write</p>
        <p>
          <md>0.000472</md>
        </p>
        <p>in scientific notation.</p>
        <table>
          <title>Scientific Notation Steps</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Move the decimal four places right.</cell>
              <cell><m>4.72</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Compensate with a power of ten.</cell>
              <cell><m>10^{-4}</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Write in scientific notation.</cell>
              <cell><m>4.72\times10^{-4}</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>4.72\times10^{-4}</md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arithmetic-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate:</p>
        <p>
          <md>8+3(5)-4.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate:</p>
        <p>
          <md>24\div6\cdot2.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute:</p>
        <p>
          <md>-7+12-9.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute:</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac58+\frac3{10}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute:</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac79-\frac16.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify:</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac{48}{72}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute:</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac34\cdot\frac8{15}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute:</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac56\div\frac{10}{9}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Solve the proportion:</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac7{12}=\frac{x}{36}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Find <m>18\%</m> of <m>250</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>A quantity decreases from <m>120</m> to <m>90</m>. Find the percent decrease.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify:</p>
        <p>
          <md>x^4x^7.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify:</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac{a^9}{a^3},\qquad a\neq0.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Rewrite without negative exponents:</p>
        <p>
          <md>x^{-4}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify:</p>
        <p>
          <md>\sqrt{75}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate:</p>
        <p>
          <md>64^{2/3}.</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Write <m>7{,}250{,}000</m> in scientific notation.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Write <m>6.3\times10^{-5}</m> in ordinary decimal notation.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why</p>
        <p>
          <md>\sqrt{a+b}=\sqrt a+\sqrt b</md>
        </p>
        <p>is not a valid general identity. Give a numerical counterexample.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why</p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac{x+4}{x}</md>
        </p>
        <p>does not permit cancellation of the <m>x</m>'s.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Explain the difference between a <m>5</m>-percentage-point increase and a <m>5\%</m> relative increase.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arithmetic-connections">
    <title>Section Connections</title>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Chapter Connection</title>
      <p>Arithmetic provides the computational and structural foundation for the number systems developed in the section on number systems. Properties such as commutativity, associativity, identity, inverses, and distributivity later become central ideas in algebra. Fractions become rational numbers and rational expressions; exponents and radicals develop into power functions; ratios and rates appear throughout geometry, probability, statistics, calculus, and applied mathematics.</p>
    </assemblage>

  </subsection>

</section>